 {:db/id "q-35906" :question/id #uuid "2c2420eb-a369-5201-ac95-195a1c7fe310" :question/math-academy-id "q-35906" :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the hyperbolic paraboloid $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=z+2$ and the plane \n$y=3$  onto the $xz$-plane is a parabola. What is the vertex of this parabola?" :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $y=3$, we substitute $y=3$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =z+2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{9} & =z+2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-1 & =z+2 \\\\ z+3 & =\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4} \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+1)^{2}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a parabola in the $xz$-plane that has its vertex at $(-1,-3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-35906/ma-source-field/f6eab300cd85129d8eb7a6be10010c152c54a7db24f3bd0f6c83213cfb4a7e68"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
