 {:db/id "q-36107" :question/id #uuid "e6d1bbdf-cfcb-54f3-b97f-c63787cc6751" :question/math-academy-id "q-36107" :question/problem "Identify the surface $4{y}^{2}+8y+9{z}^{2}-5x+9=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square in the $y$ term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{2}+8y+9{z}^{2}-5x+9 & =0 \\\\ 4({y}^{2}+2y)+9{z}^{2}-5x+9 & =0 \\\\ 4[(y+1)^{2}-1]+9{z}^{2}-5x+9 & =0 \\\\ 4(y+1)^{2}-4+9{z}^{2}-5x+9 & =0 \\\\ 4(y+1)^{2}+9{z}^{2}-5x+5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, by rearranging, we get the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(y+1)^{2}+9{z}^{2}-5x+5 & =0 \\\\ 4(y+1)^{2}+9{z}^{2} & =5(x-1) \\\\ \\frac{4(y+1)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{9{z}^{2}}{5} & =x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of an elliptic paraboloid centered at $(1,-1,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-36107/ma-source-field/0ac80f1cfcd05e529a26e0ceb2d2b4e97d4e29cf49f5d9f425041d98ac068771"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
