 {:db/id "q-37088/ma-source-field/3dfbe00649f6bdc41b9d4e08683b744ce6c8eefbefea47f2f7a76fac11b38ad1" :answer-field/id #uuid "c165be63-7aaf-561e-9d48-4500807ec533" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-37088/ma-source-field/3dfbe00649f6bdc41b9d4e08683b744ce6c8eefbefea47f2f7a76fac11b38ad1/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" "q-37088/ma-source-field/3dfbe00649f6bdc41b9d4e08683b744ce6c8eefbefea47f2f7a76fac11b38ad1/48ebbdc9809f7db521b278c0fd14937bfb7024f97bbcab1451a89717374ca921" "q-37088/ma-source-field/3dfbe00649f6bdc41b9d4e08683b744ce6c8eefbefea47f2f7a76fac11b38ad1/4e89fef022c66e8791ad8a91f77132b4a3693400c6c7688e4b516485c98df597" "q-37088/ma-source-field/3dfbe00649f6bdc41b9d4e08683b744ce6c8eefbefea47f2f7a76fac11b38ad1/97d7485a9bfdcf93bb0dd4fdd387979176cc451e9b1a3cdb3e642af89543a21b" "q-37088/ma-source-field/3dfbe00649f6bdc41b9d4e08683b744ce6c8eefbefea47f2f7a76fac11b38ad1/8f36d211ba516e1541b14f795d57bbee38407cf4718a20efdfe45422d50f6db5"] :answer-field/correct "q-37088/ma-source-field/3dfbe00649f6bdc41b9d4e08683b744ce6c8eefbefea47f2f7a76fac11b38ad1/97d7485a9bfdcf93bb0dd4fdd387979176cc451e9b1a3cdb3e642af89543a21b" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-37088" :question/id #uuid "73d12e91-b996-5b04-bc26-e6f1ba8817f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-37088" :question/problem "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $A=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ using its characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-6-\\lambda  & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-6-\\lambda )(8-\\lambda )-(8)(-3) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -24 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}]-(-4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-37088/ma-source-field/3dfbe00649f6bdc41b9d4e08683b744ce6c8eefbefea47f2f7a76fac11b38ad1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
