 {:db/id "q-37275/ma-source-field/3147ae14c018b687fe8383dfcca31992a0226b3a70833b2a1e7a46050044f1af" :answer-field/id #uuid "86178ef2-46a4-5efa-9560-b3d46ddc8abd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-37275/ma-source-field/3147ae14c018b687fe8383dfcca31992a0226b3a70833b2a1e7a46050044f1af/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" "q-37275/ma-source-field/3147ae14c018b687fe8383dfcca31992a0226b3a70833b2a1e7a46050044f1af/9095eaea1a755bb68d03f02b41d35a3c49612fae7842182e213ae3543ba51927" "q-37275/ma-source-field/3147ae14c018b687fe8383dfcca31992a0226b3a70833b2a1e7a46050044f1af/7a1ff3f324f4881c81b042ff2d543bafb0d2889d1e1d89d620bb2f3523f23f38" "q-37275/ma-source-field/3147ae14c018b687fe8383dfcca31992a0226b3a70833b2a1e7a46050044f1af/77b76f62f68ac8a5132a6bd68e4c546d0c5253dbbb1ae8f974f56879711a06e0" "q-37275/ma-source-field/3147ae14c018b687fe8383dfcca31992a0226b3a70833b2a1e7a46050044f1af/d8cf46fa14910a57ea25cb889d8fca97c275be8a7c9ae6eb90e71cfd3e34f69e"] :answer-field/correct "q-37275/ma-source-field/3147ae14c018b687fe8383dfcca31992a0226b3a70833b2a1e7a46050044f1af/77b76f62f68ac8a5132a6bd68e4c546d0c5253dbbb1ae8f974f56879711a06e0" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "a033518a-f4fb-5fd7-9472-bd2a3557ddc1"] :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $A=PD{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we find the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 6 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-37275/ma-source-field/3147ae14c018b687fe8383dfcca31992a0226b3a70833b2a1e7a46050044f1af"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
