 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "784d8995-a39a-5567-b522-960291c7a1e9"] :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+6xy+2$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x+6y & 2y+6x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x+6y=0 \\\\ 2y+6x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+6(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+2 \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2 \\\\  & =4+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (4+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{′} & =0 \\\\ 12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain five candidate points:\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt{2},0),\\,(1,1),\\,(-1,1),\\,(-1,-1),\\,(1,-1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(\\sqrt{2},0)$ | $(1,1)$ | $(-1,1)$ | $(-1,-1)$ | $(1,-1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $4$ | $10$ | $-2$ | $10$ | $-2$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2$ at the points $(-1,1)$ and $(1,-1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $10$ at the points $(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-37372/ma-source-field/0e34e892e13973fc6581cb14429e0a00090b7ddf3a5da95ba6e17fd11aa3215f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
