 {:db/id "q-37633/ma-source-field/481d103dcd4aff18f5686079fd867678de24d48089f1baef6eccf7b0dceac70c" :answer-field/id #uuid "48c93eb5-5578-5226-8ad3-ab9b9a9ba7af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-37633/ma-source-field/481d103dcd4aff18f5686079fd867678de24d48089f1baef6eccf7b0dceac70c/2caf8873a0b6230d1da4479e09489513e971f6209e4cc0bf9afd97773ef077b2" "q-37633/ma-source-field/481d103dcd4aff18f5686079fd867678de24d48089f1baef6eccf7b0dceac70c/142bd5071c112b26b648fff75ad587ac55f88aaf316243c4090e7b46cb78b6e2" "q-37633/ma-source-field/481d103dcd4aff18f5686079fd867678de24d48089f1baef6eccf7b0dceac70c/60dd8adab7710ba6d8d4f722c3d4b1e159712baa09d78b56abd178a8243b23d8" "q-37633/ma-source-field/481d103dcd4aff18f5686079fd867678de24d48089f1baef6eccf7b0dceac70c/899617056192d1022bfac5cf0d82bc941a5f88c83e5b1243a2de3a4bb0f2ed11" "q-37633/ma-source-field/481d103dcd4aff18f5686079fd867678de24d48089f1baef6eccf7b0dceac70c/7a834c0309a4a41915541ad01f284bffc09fb0bd57161b01cd0b42e03c2513d6"] :answer-field/correct "q-37633/ma-source-field/481d103dcd4aff18f5686079fd867678de24d48089f1baef6eccf7b0dceac70c/60dd8adab7710ba6d8d4f722c3d4b1e159712baa09d78b56abd178a8243b23d8" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-37633" :question/id #uuid "a4b19937-90d1-5717-b7a0-7b53ad9d3dcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-37633" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}BP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=7\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-5$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $B.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=7,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-5)I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]+5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-37633/ma-source-field/481d103dcd4aff18f5686079fd867678de24d48089f1baef6eccf7b0dceac70c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
