 {:db/id "q-37774" :question/id #uuid "ae2d41a6-3045-5117-8d5e-3a9ab6755a10" :question/math-academy-id "q-37774" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form. The system corresponding to $M$ is \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+0y+3z=7 \\\\ 0x+y+2z=-3 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just two equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3z=7 \\\\ y+2z=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ To do this, we apply the back substitution. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+3z=7\\,⇒\\,x=7-3z\n$$\n\nand from the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\ny+2z=-3\\,⇒\\,y=-3-2z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=7-3z \\\\ y=-3-2z \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7-3z \\\\ -3-2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-37774/ma-source-field/058a431472f0d6d992d456fc796990980f384dd4eda49e4809becf163a1c56ed"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
