 {:db/id "q-37853/ma-source-field/20881247302120b2c2e818d31ff449e18d167c25cf6322902491733f68299b51" :answer-field/id #uuid "97159dc8-c246-515e-a270-dbbd6b9b5ec3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-37853/ma-source-field/20881247302120b2c2e818d31ff449e18d167c25cf6322902491733f68299b51/af9c91f42bb8fa5b8b8d5e5abca894084aeaf53db0e01f1114e505251c0e4550" "q-37853/ma-source-field/20881247302120b2c2e818d31ff449e18d167c25cf6322902491733f68299b51/04b43304665c990807f6195a14d59cd31f60760cd7e94a9a2744fe6f93f7fa17" "q-37853/ma-source-field/20881247302120b2c2e818d31ff449e18d167c25cf6322902491733f68299b51/d0a73f4c5845ca651fb9714d7559d3bbb079c6bcb18a0b88885ee97544881315" "q-37853/ma-source-field/20881247302120b2c2e818d31ff449e18d167c25cf6322902491733f68299b51/63cdde4d9fcdebbc44c4ad29929d07dff57d67794c3a789de38fd17e431dc268" "q-37853/ma-source-field/20881247302120b2c2e818d31ff449e18d167c25cf6322902491733f68299b51/e37756d6fed25faaa08413d4f0918af9b55b7c894b3c5b5e4f93463177dc41c0"] :answer-field/correct "q-37853/ma-source-field/20881247302120b2c2e818d31ff449e18d167c25cf6322902491733f68299b51/d0a73f4c5845ca651fb9714d7559d3bbb079c6bcb18a0b88885ee97544881315" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-37853" :question/id #uuid "197a265b-1aed-5f18-a576-60c74ff63df1" :question/math-academy-id "q-37853" :question/problem "$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $B$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}BP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we get the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-2I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-(-2)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-4I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-37853/ma-source-field/20881247302120b2c2e818d31ff449e18d167c25cf6322902491733f68299b51"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
