 {:db/id "q-38020" :question/id #uuid "f8007798-26fd-5f48-9d8f-a07b9c92e47f" :question/math-academy-id "q-38020" :question/problem "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of $\\,{R}^{2}.$ If $T$ is a linear transformation such that $T(i)=i$ and $T(j)=3j,$ then what is the geometric interpretation of the transformation $T?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the unit square spanned by the vectors $\\,i\\,$ and  $\\,j.$\n\n![](images/f2/f2438da7f6f0a2ab7e6653cad7776ef7d01faba55d22a480a98c152e81f0d78a.png)\n\nNow, using the fact that $T(i)=i$ and $T(j)=3j,$ we can draw the image of the square under the transformation $T:$\n\n![](images/86/8674e7345c903bf193e09cc9ae1702383cad10ba06c5dc3b6d2713ea30eb843e.png)\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch with a scale factor $3$ in the $y$-direction." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-38020/ma-source-field/f7273419be3f8fb552b354a01763d4419d4c4b763b9f28f6ba57cde813190e61"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
