 {:db/id "q-38187/ma-source-field/fa3711b762b1b8adfd81faa5b6483beea2f8c6b4450c518c9896327dc2e40ebd" :answer-field/id #uuid "22d26b64-8f4f-5847-bc42-645a93a0286d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-38187/ma-source-field/fa3711b762b1b8adfd81faa5b6483beea2f8c6b4450c518c9896327dc2e40ebd/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-38187/ma-source-field/fa3711b762b1b8adfd81faa5b6483beea2f8c6b4450c518c9896327dc2e40ebd/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-38187/ma-source-field/fa3711b762b1b8adfd81faa5b6483beea2f8c6b4450c518c9896327dc2e40ebd/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-38187/ma-source-field/fa3711b762b1b8adfd81faa5b6483beea2f8c6b4450c518c9896327dc2e40ebd/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-38187/ma-source-field/fa3711b762b1b8adfd81faa5b6483beea2f8c6b4450c518c9896327dc2e40ebd/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :answer-field/correct "q-38187/ma-source-field/fa3711b762b1b8adfd81faa5b6483beea2f8c6b4450c518c9896327dc2e40ebd/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-38187" :question/id #uuid "a5eb8a6b-37c6-54e6-b340-18101dbf3f8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-38187" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $N$ given below?\n\nI. $N$ is **not** a singular matrix\nII. $N$ is a singular matrix\nIII. $det(N)=-1$\n\n$$\nN=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7} & 3 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & 4 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & 5 & -\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7} & 3 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & 4 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & 5 & -\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}|\\begin{aligned}4 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ 5 & -\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7} & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & -\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}|+(-\\frac{1}{7})|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7} & 4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(-\\frac{4}{7}+\\frac{5}{7})-3(-\\frac{1}{49}+\\frac{1}{49})-\\frac{1}{7}(\\frac{5}{7}-\\frac{4}{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{7}-3\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{49}-\\frac{1}{49} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(N)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-38187/ma-source-field/fa3711b762b1b8adfd81faa5b6483beea2f8c6b4450c518c9896327dc2e40ebd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
