 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "2aeb7023-942d-59e4-9752-79902203013d"] :question/problem "The size of a bush growing in a schoolyard is being monitored. The area $A\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}$ of the bush at any given moment grows at a rate that's directly proportional to the area at that moment. Additionally, $2\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}$ of the bush is cut away every month.\nWhat is the differential equation that describes the rate of change in the area of the schoolyard bush at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time in months since it started growing?" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change in the area of the bush per month increases in proportion to $A$ and decreases by $2.$ The differential equation that describes this is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=kA-2,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the (positive) constant of proportionality." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-38324/ma-source-field/beb09f754a1f078400435491ebb4991d4c8db14843ece0287e16500067621f2b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
