 {:db/id "q-38569/ma-source-field/dbf85f35325befcf505b845bc5a083096ab24869fb1e312bf7d9376ba85cec56" :answer-field/id #uuid "8d34fd1c-9293-5e59-b2bf-8bb681a518f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-38569/ma-source-field/dbf85f35325befcf505b845bc5a083096ab24869fb1e312bf7d9376ba85cec56/19d2044ffdf8e8bd0ad7b2b19785f5e61c692963e11aac0cf5bc39a0a1765cad" "q-38569/ma-source-field/dbf85f35325befcf505b845bc5a083096ab24869fb1e312bf7d9376ba85cec56/2cf9dbcff1e9e13d5cb7523331870096970711f217fd94272657650a88f160eb" "q-38569/ma-source-field/dbf85f35325befcf505b845bc5a083096ab24869fb1e312bf7d9376ba85cec56/029dd98b56f78d08fa8a6fde374699cfefdf578d7527402287df2caf0687c4b5" "q-38569/ma-source-field/dbf85f35325befcf505b845bc5a083096ab24869fb1e312bf7d9376ba85cec56/c2a406d0c47a0d7fe087d0ef3e0e5cb0e9a59b6cc7a95feafc573ea7a50e2324" "q-38569/ma-source-field/dbf85f35325befcf505b845bc5a083096ab24869fb1e312bf7d9376ba85cec56/3f377310e752ed776614ea94941e8c6aef2905f3e7297cc9eed32c193c48bf18"] :answer-field/correct "q-38569/ma-source-field/dbf85f35325befcf505b845bc5a083096ab24869fb1e312bf7d9376ba85cec56/029dd98b56f78d08fa8a6fde374699cfefdf578d7527402287df2caf0687c4b5" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-38569" :question/id #uuid "5efb10ac-d6d7-5644-9104-60d88e699342" :question/math-academy-id "q-38569" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -(-2) & 1\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=1$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-2.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =1+(-2)\\text{i}=1-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}0+1\\text{i} \\\\ 2+0\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =1∓2\\text{i},\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-38569/ma-source-field/dbf85f35325befcf505b845bc5a083096ab24869fb1e312bf7d9376ba85cec56"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
