 {:db/id "q-38715" :question/id #uuid "8f9c9f1a-022c-5394-a51c-b1a490ff1e16" :question/math-academy-id "q-38715" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)$ below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{3}, & x<0 \\\\ {x}^{6}+1, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nI. It has a removable discontinuity at $x=0.$\nII. It has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$\nIII. It's continuous at $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "For $x=0,$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(0)={0}^{6}+1=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{(x-1)}^{3}=(0-1)^{3}=-1\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}({x}^{6}+1)={0}^{6}+1=1.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ \n\nHence, I is false, II is true and III is false." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-38715/ma-source-field/e6174fc4e75787c26e8f0cb1c1a7fc12511d781a72501b525c6cfee3049e17f7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
