 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "a0d796fd-8159-54fb-b817-7bb654ebaf53"] :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -(-2) & 5\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=5$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-2.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =5+(-2)\\text{i}=5-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1+4\\text{i} \\\\ 3+0\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1+4\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =5∓2\\text{i},\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-1\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-39130/ma-source-field/ce5906d12826ba36cb1f52bad715865b6f07dfbb8378eaac546ac3ab7ba7a6e0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
