 {:db/id "q-40179" :question/id #uuid "4bfe7ea3-d20b-5a92-b7ac-e6c2ec81d721" :question/math-academy-id "q-40179" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation\n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}6x-4y \\\\ 2x-y \\\\ -5x+3y\\end{aligned}]$ and the vectors \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the given vectors belong to $\\text{Ker}(T)?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{i})\\ne 0$ for any $i$'s, we conclude that none of them belongs to the kernel of $\\,T.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-40179/ma-source-field/6f24747ab7946961875fc409dc23aeb1e5ecc83c1969b8fad10f4bc2662f77c3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
