 {:db/id "q-40277" :question/id #uuid "5d6af0ce-2d7e-5e7e-9759-710fbc03ce1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-40277" :question/problem "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\nI. $4+3+\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{27}{16}+⋯$\nII. $16-2+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{32}+⋯$\nIII. $-8+16-32+64-⋯$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- For the geometric series $4+3+\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{27}{16}+⋯$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $16-2+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{32}+⋯$ the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{-2}{16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- For the geometric series $-8+16-32+64-⋯$  the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{16}{-8} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series I and II converge." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-40277/ma-source-field/8258a0f10194a6ed5db20710e030cca02d2b41122fa3d4b0c2a07fbf3b80d6b7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
